  - Joint-family duties
:::

### 7. The burden of unpaid care

#### i) The scale
Women do about **three times** as much unpaid care work as men worldwide (ILO, ==2018==) — cooking, cleaning, caring for children, the sick and the elderly.

#### ii) The double burden
Women who take paid jobs keep their unpaid work: **Arlie Hochschild** named this the "**second shift**" (==1989==). **Caroline Moser** described women's **triple role** — productive, reproductive and community-managing.

#### iii) An example
A village teacher earns a salary but still cooks, cleans and cares for children and in-laws before and after school; without childcare, transport or help at home, many such women stop work after marriage or a first child.

#### iv) Why it is invisible
Unpaid care is excluded from GDP — **Marilyn Waring** (*If Women Counted*, ==1988==) — so policy ignores it. **SDG target 5.4** calls for it to be recognised, reduced and shared.

### 8. Why women's work matters

#### i) For women
Paid work gives **income, independence, networks and voice**. **Naila Kabeer** (*The Power to Choose*, ==2000==) found that Bangladeshi garment work gave many women their first independent income and greater bargaining power.

#### ii) For the economy
A country that keeps three-quarters of its women out of the labour force wastes half its talent and loses growth.

### 9. What exists

#### i) Laws
The harassment law (==2010==), maternity and paternity leave (==2020==), the **Sindh Home-Based Workers Act 2018** and the **Punjab Domestic Workers Act 2019**.

#### ii) Quotas
A **10%** quota for women in federal jobs — though many posts remain unfilled, and the private sector has no equivalent.

### 10. Policies 1: mobility, safety and care

#### i) Safe transport
More women-only and safe public transport, lighting, and enforcement against street harassment — the change that would raise participation **fastest**.

#### ii) Childcare
Public and workplace **daycare**, required for large employers and government offices.

#### iii) Shared care
Paid **paternity leave** and campaigns for men's share of housework; **time-use surveys** to count care.

### 11. Policies 2: pay, protection and skills

#### i) Fair pay
**Equal pay for work of equal value** (comparable worth) and enforced minimum wages.

#### ii) Protect informal workers
Extend social security and minimum wages to **home-based and domestic workers** in every province.

#### iii) Skills and digital work
Vocational and **digital** training, using the narrowing **mobile-internet gender gap** (38% to 25%, GSMA ==2024–25==) for online work.

#### iv) Enforce and lead
Strict enforcement of the harassment law in private firms, and more women in management and on boards.

### 12. Conclusion

#### i) The diagnosis
Pakistan's female LFPR of 24.4% reflects not women's choices but a system: informal and low-paid work, norms of honour and breadwinning, unsafe transport, scarce childcare and a crushing burden of unpaid care.

#### ii) The way forward
The fastest gains would come from **safe transport, childcare, enforced harassment law, protected home-based and digital work and equal pay**. Making paid work compatible with care — and sharing care with men — is the key that unlocks the rest.

## Why this answer scores
- Reads the stem's **24.4%** in context (trend, provinces, global rank of 144th).
- Uses the question's own categories — **economic, social, institutional** — as headings, with **unpaid care** separate.
- Grounds the analysis in theory (Hochschild, Moser, Waring, Kabeer, Mies).
- Proposes **specific policies**, identifying which would work fastest.
- A **tree** figure maps the barriers.
